  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1126 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn upper level disturbance moving across the region through\nthe overnight hours will bring a mix of light rain or \nlight freezing rain/drizzle. Temperatures during the evening\nbottomed out in the 20s across the north and around 30 \nacross central Wisconsin. As the thicker clouds move during the\nlate evening and overnight hours, temperatures are likely to\nrebound back to around the freezing mark. It will make for \na difficult forecast determining the impacts of any freezing\nrain/drizzle over central into north-central and far northeast\nWisconsin. Precipitation amounts should be 0.05 inches or less,\nbut could lead to impacts on roads, bridges and sidewalks\nover north-central, central and far northeast WI. For the moment,\nthe most likely way to handle the situation overnight is with a \nSpecial Weather Statement if freezing precipitation does occur. \nAny lingering precipitation should around mid-morning across \neastern WI. \n\nAnother mild day is in store on Saturday with highs in the 40s.  \nNormal high is in the middle 30s to near 40. An upper trough will\nswing across the area on Sunday, bringing a chance of light snow\nto far northern Wisconsin Sunday night. Meanwhile, a system \npassing to the south of the area has shifted a little north on the\nmodel runs tonight. A little rain or freezing is possible late \nSaturday night into early Sunday morning at 15-30%. Highest rain \nchances on Sunday will rise to 45% across southern Manitowoc \nCounty, but drop off sharply to the north and west. Temperatures \nreturn closer to normal Sunday and Monday, then mild temperatures \nwill prevail Tuesday into next weekend. It could become more \nactive Friday into next weekend, but it is too early to get too \nexcited on the rain/snow potential. \n\nAs for the climate books, November will go down in the record\nbooks considerably warmer than normal which is typical during\nan ongoing El Nino. Precipitation/snowfall totals will be below \nnormal.",
